### Audit/accounting, resource, and compatibility evidence

- The genuine accepted fake path records reservation, reserved commitment,
  started commitment, and reconciliation in that order and still sends exactly
  one all-R1 fake request. Definite before-send, uncertain-start, authoritative
  response, exact terminal-code, fresh restore, canonical plan/pricing/source,
  and legacy schema-v1 behavior remain covered by the exact focus.
- The recovery no-mutation assertion captures calls, prompt/completion/total and
  reserved tokens, float and Decimal reserved USD, spent/authoritative/retained
  USD, retained tokens, incomplete-accounting count, active reservations, and
  reconciliation maps before and after rejected evidence.
- Provider/budget/receipt evidence remains closed lifecycle, model, pricing,
  accounting, reservation, and digest data. No prompt, policy source, raw model
  response, credential, header, or operator approval is persisted or reported.
- Every client, response, pricing loader, and SDK constructor was injected and
  fake. Every ledger/audit path was under pytest temporary roots. The exact
  foreground commands exited; no provider transmission, live pricing fetch,
  real SDK construction, permanent ledger, replay/parity job, Docker, helper
  process, upload, push, merge, or cloud action occurred.

### Remaining concerns

- Live provider/SDK compatibility remains intentionally untested at this safety
  boundary.
- Pre-Round-5 optimizer terminal histories do not contain the new trusted
  gateway commitment and therefore cannot be auto-resealed; they fail closed
  and require an explicit trusted migration or terminal disposition. Legacy
  schema-v1 provider records and standalone authorization accounting remain
  supported.
- Verification noise is limited to the pre-existing unknown `cache_dir` warning.
